How to properly size a thread pool ?

- num of cores
- the workload type

Num of cores
- Lets say we have 4 cpu cores and a thread scheduler responsible for dispatching threads on cpu. Say,
we have 3 threads and a Task queue which threads can grab task from it.
- If we have num of threads less than num of CPUs, system will be udner used
- CPU utilization percantage can help see this
- On the other hand if we have too many threads, scheduler will be overwhelmed because it has to
do lot of context switching between threads and its internal DS will grow with num of threads.
- System spends more time on context switching instead of actual work
- performance will suffer
- Another angle is workload type - CPU heavy, task needs CPU to be executed - Math,control flow, encryption algo, search and sort
- other workload type is i/o heavy or i/o bound - tasks requires external resource to network. Any ops involving n/w,
Http api access, DB access, Disk access, File/Dir access
- third type is mixed workload - Both CPU and I/O. 

CPU heavy tasks
- right size is thread pool should be equal to num of CPU cores
- workload needs CPU to complete, allocation needs to avoid contention or underusability
- we increase the probability for a thread to be scheduled on CPU core, increase time a thread runs on cpu
- don't set too much pressure on the scheduler

I/O heavy tasks
- 4 threads and all talking to DB. Threads will mostly have to wait for DB to ack the request
- Threads don't need CPU, they only need DB to respond fast to maximize throughput
- We can increase threads to get more throughput. We can then take twice as many requests and dispatch them.
- Bottleneck will be DB layer, num of connections DB supports.
- Don't oversize threadpools, limit should be on num of inbound connections for external service
- Num of threads > Num of CPU cores 

Mixed workload
- can't have a rule of thumb as it depends on the application
- Split the workloads into multiple thread pools, one per workload-type
- one pool for encryption, one for api calls, etc and size them differently depending on load
- Try to get the size of thread-pools empirically
- Num of threads = (num of cpu cores) * (1 + wait time/service time)
wait time - time that external service takes to respond back to application
service time - time for your app to do other processing for that request apart from wait time eg. serializn, deserialization

Eg.
REST API responds in 30ms
Processing time is 5ms
Num of CPU cores = 8

8*(1+30/5) = 56 threads
bottleneck is REST API itself

Follow the guidelines but do your own testing





























